      <div class="msv__content">
        <p>If you&#x27;re planning to study Spanish in Spain, you need a language school that is officially recognized and will provide an acceptance letter that satisfies visa requirements. Not all language schools qualify, and choosing the wrong one can result in visa rejection. Here&#x27;s what you need to know about finding a legitimate school and ensuring your acceptance letter holds weight with Spanish consulates.</p>
<h2>What Makes a Language School Eligible for a Student Visa?</h2>
<p>Official Registration and Accreditation</p>
<p>Your language school must be officially registered with Spain&#x27;s regional education authorities (Consejerías de Educación). It should hold certifications from bodies like EAQUALS (European Association for Quality Language Services) or CCHS (Cámara de Comercio). Schools without official registration cannot issue valid acceptance letters.</p>
<p>Structured, Long-Term Programs</p>
<p>Visa-eligible programs must be formal, with defined curricula, schedules, and durations. Short weekend courses or drop-in classes don&#x27;t qualify. Most consulates require a minimum 20-30 hours per week and programs lasting at least 3-6 months. The longer your program, the stronger your visa application.</p>
<p>Ability to Verify Enrollment</p>
<p>Consulates verify directly with language schools. The school must be responsive, maintain proper records, and issue detailed acceptance letters. Schools that are difficult to reach or provide vague documentation will damage your visa application.</p>
<h2>Characteristics of Reputable Spanish Language Schools</h2>
<p>Established Track Record</p>
<p>Schools operating for 10+ years with consistent student enrollment are safer bets. Newer, unproven schools are viewed with suspicion by consulates. Check online reviews, ask about their immigration compliance history, and confirm they have helped students secure visas before.</p>
<p>Professional Administrative Support</p>
<p>Quality schools assign you a dedicated contact, provide clear documentation of your enrollment and progress, and handle visa-related requests promptly. They understand visa requirements and proactively provide information students need.</p>
<p>Transparent Fee Structure</p>
<p>The cost and payment terms should be clear and realistic. Schools that charge suspiciously low fees or have unclear payment policies are red flags. Your acceptance letter should specify exactly what you&#x27;re paying for and when.</p>
<p>Qualified Instructors</p>
<p>Teachers should hold DELE (Diploma de Español como Lengua Extranjera) certification or university degrees in Spanish language/education. Class sizes should be small (8-12 students maximum) to ensure quality. Ask about teacher qualifications before enrolling.</p>
<p>Unsure which language school will satisfy visa requirements?</p>
<p>We can help you evaluate schools, confirm their eligibility, and ensure your enrollment documentation is complete and persuasive for your visa application.</p>
